  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Fraudbusters: code walkthrough for the judges",
  "description": "Click Next to jump through the code. Each box says what the code does and what to tell the judges.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Welcome (read this first)",
      "description": "## Fraudbusters code walkthrough\n\nThis tour jumps to each important piece of code. The yellow highlight shows the lines to point at.\n\n* **Next →** (bottom right of this box) goes to the next step.\n* **SAY** = what to tell the judges.\n* Grey command lines are buttons: click them to run the code in the terminal.\n\n### How the code fits together\n\n```\ndata/generate_synthetic.py   makes bank transactions with planted laundering\n        ↓\nsrc/aml.py                   THE BRAIN: graph + 4 detectors + risk score\n        ↓\napp/server.py                backend (FastAPI): sends alerts to the website\n        ↓\napp/web/app.js               frontend: graph, replay, case board\n```\n\n**SAY:** \"Our project has three layers: a detection engine in Python, a FastAPI backend, and a JavaScript frontend. Let me walk you through each.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "data/generate_synthetic.py",
      "line": 4,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 22,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "1. Where the data comes from",
      "description": "Real bank data is confidential, so we generate a realistic bank: 3,000 accounts **with KYC details** (account type, opening date, owner), normal payments, and two kinds of planted cases:\n\n* **200 laundering schemes** (fan in, fan out, cycle, mule chain)\n* **137 GENUINE look-alikes** with the same shapes: donation drives, relief redistribution, payroll, chit funds, merchant sales, people moving money between their own accounts\n\n**SAY:** \"The real challenge is not finding shapes, it is telling crime apart from genuine activity with the same shape, like a donation drive versus smurfing. So our data deliberately contains both.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 21,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 21,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 32,
          "character": 80
        }
      },
      "title": "2. The detection engine: rule settings",
      "description": "This is the **main file**. These numbers are the rules every detector follows, for example:\n\n* `FAN_WINDOW_H = 72` → look at 72 hour windows\n* `FAN_MIN_PARTIES = 8` → at least 8 different people\n* `CHAIN_MAX_GAP_H = 6` → a mule forwards money within 6 hours\n* `PASS_RATIO` → each hop keeps 90 to 100% of the money\n\n**SAY:** \"All our rules are in one place at the top, so a bank can tune them without touching the logic.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 77,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 77,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 82,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "3. Building the graph",
      "description": "Every **account becomes a node** and every **transfer becomes an arrow** (edge) carrying its amount and time. We use the NetworkX library.\n\n**SAY:** \"This is the core idea. Banks look at one transaction at a time. We connect them all into a graph, so patterns across many accounts become visible.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 108,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 96,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 127,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "4. Detector 1 and 2: fan in and fan out (smurfing)",
      "description": "For every account we go through the money it received (fan in) or sent (fan out) in time order.\n\nLine 108 is a **sliding window**: two pointers `i` and `j` keep only transfers within 72 hours of each other. If 8 or more different accounts fall inside, it is suspicious.\n\n**SAY:** \"Smurfing means splitting money into many small deposits into one account. We slide a 72 hour window over each account's history and count how many different senders appear. The two pointer window makes it fast: each transfer is visited only once.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 89,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 86,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 93,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "5. How suspicious is the fan? (score)",
      "description": "The score goes up when:\n* there are many senders (`party_term`)\n* amounts sit **just under the ₹10,000 reporting limit** (`near_limit`, line 89): classic structuring\n* amounts are very similar (`uniformity`)\n* the total is large (`big`)\n\n**SAY:** \"Launderers keep each deposit just below the reporting limit to avoid checks. Line 89 detects exactly that.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 152,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 139,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 156,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "6. Speed trick: following money fast",
      "description": "To follow money we constantly ask: **where did this money go next?**\n\n`_OutIndex` keeps each account's outgoing transfers sorted by time, so line 152 can use **binary search** (`bisect`) instead of scanning all 41,000 rows.\n\n**SAY:** \"Following money hop by hop could be slow. We index outgoing transfers by time and use binary search, so each lookup is instant.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 174,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 164,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 187,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "7. Detector 3: round trip cycles",
      "description": "From every transfer we **follow the money** hop by hop (a depth first search with a `stack`). A hop only counts if it happens within 48 hours and keeps almost the full amount.\n\nLine 174: if the money **arrives back where it started** after 3 or more hops, it is a cycle.\n\n**SAY:** \"Round tripping sends money in a loop to fake business turnover. We follow each transfer forward, and if the money comes back to its origin with almost nothing lost, we flag it.\"\n\nTip: click left of line 174 to set a red breakpoint, then run configuration 2 with F5 to pause here live."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 190,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 190,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 230,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "8. Detector 4: mule chains",
      "description": "Same follow the money search, but looking for the **longest straight line** of accounts that each pass the money on within 6 hours, keeping 90 to 100%.\n\nIt runs twice: fast mules, and **patient mules** (slower hops, but the amount must match almost exactly).\n\n**SAY:** \"Money mules are accounts that receive money and immediately forward it. We find the longest such chain, and we also catch patient mules who wait up to 2 days.\""
    },
    {
      "file": "src/aml.py",
      "line": 239,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 233,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 244,
          "character": 1
        }
      },
      "title": "9. Risk score 0 to 100",
      "description": "Line 239 is the formula:\n\n**40%** how cleanly the pattern matches + **25%** money involved + **20%** speed + **15%** number of accounts, times a weight per typology.\n\nAlerts are sorted by risk and named AL0001, AL0002, …\n\n**SAY:** \"Investigators have limited time, so every alert gets a risk score and the queue is sorted with the most dangerous case first.\""
    },
    {
